<h2>What was customized</h2>

<p><strong>Stock movement.</strong> Material Receipt, Transfer, Issue and Return flows were configured so the digital record follows the physical movement of goods through the factory.</p>

<p><strong>Batch and LOT traceability.</strong> Custom batch fields capture the operational details needed to identify and locate fabric, including party, order, pantone, received quantity, rolls, stock status and days in stock.</p>

<p><strong>Gate passes.</strong> Dyeing and inward movement use digital gate-pass workflows instead of isolated handwritten records. This connects external processing to the same traceability model.</p>

<p><strong>Critical Path and order visibility.</strong> Production stages are tracked in one place so status can be read from the system rather than reconstructed from separate department updates.</p>

<p><strong>Reports and workspaces.</strong> Custom reports such as the Fabric Stock Register expose live operational data. Frappe desk and workspace customization keeps the relevant tasks visible without forcing staff through unrelated ERP modules.</p>

<p><strong>Automation.</strong> Client scripts set safe defaults and validate common Stock Entry paths. Server methods create and recalculate batches, maintain status through cancellation and enforce item-level constraints. Barcode-supported store workflows reduce manual lookup during receipts and returns.</p>

<h2>Implementation constraints</h2>

<p>The main constraint is adoption. A technically correct workflow fails if it adds friction on the factory floor. Screens and defaults are tested against the real task, then simplified until the digital path is at least as direct as the paper path it replaces.</p>

<p>Privacy is another constraint. Buyer names, suppliers, prices, internal orders and staff identities belong inside the system, not in a public case study. The workflow diagram describes the architecture without exposing operational records.</p>

<h2>Result</h2>

<p>Material movement, batch traceability, gate passes, order progress and stock reporting now share one operational system. The work continues as production needs change. Planned ideas remain separate from shipped functionality.</p>

<h2>Lesson</h2>

<blockquote><p>ERP implementation is modeling work: understand how material moves, encode it clearly, then make the resulting workflow easier to follow than the old one.</p></blockquote>

